  "answer_text": "## Why Does `new_list = my_list` Affect the Original?\n\n### The Root Cause: References, Not Copies\n\nAssignment statements in Python **do not copy objects** \u2014 they create **bindings** between a target and an object. This means that in Python, assignment never creates a new object for mutable types like lists. Instead, both variables point to the **same underlying data in memory**.\n\nSo when you write:\n```python\nnew_list = my_list\n```\nassignment makes the two variables point to the **one list in memory**, so if you edit the new list, changes will be reflected on the original list.\n\nYou can verify this using `id()`:\n```python\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3]\nnew_list = my_list\nprint(id(my_list) == id(new_list))  # True \u2014 same object in memory!\n```\n\n---\n\n## How to Properly Copy a List\n\nThere are three main approaches, depending on your needs:\n\n### 1. \u2705 Shallow Copy (for flat/simple lists)\n\nThere are two main types of copies in Python: shallow copies and deep copies. A shallow copy creates a new object, but the nested objects within it still refer to the same objects as the original. In the case of a list, a shallow copy creates a new list, but the elements of the new list are references to the same objects as in the original list.\n\nFor flat lists, a shallow copy is sufficient and can be done in multiple ways:\n\n**a) Using `.copy()` method:**\n```python\nnew_list = my_list.copy()\n```\n\n**b) Using slicing `[:]`:**\n```python\nnew_list = my_list[:]\n```\n\n**c) Using `list()` constructor:**\n```python\nnew_list = list(my_list)\n```\n\nShallow copies (`.copy()`, `list()`, slicing) are sufficient for flat lists.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \u2705 Deep Copy (for nested/complex lists)\n\nIf your list contains **nested lists or other mutable objects**, a shallow copy won't fully protect you. The difference between shallow and deep copying is only relevant for compound objects (objects that contain other objects, like lists or class instances). A shallow copy constructs a new compound object and then inserts references into it to the objects found in the original. A deep copy constructs a new compound object and then, recursively, inserts copies into it of the objects found in the original.\n\nUse `copy.deepcopy()` for nested structures:\n```python\nimport copy\nnew_list = copy.deepcopy(my_list)\n```\n\nThe deep copy is different from a shallow copy in that the copied elements have their own pointers and are not referenced to the original elements. Therefore, no matter how you modify the deep copy, the changes will **not** be reflected on the original list.\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **Note:** Creating a deep copy is slower, because you are making new copies of everything. Deep copies can also be memory-intensive, especially for large and complex data structures, so only use them when absolutely necessary.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Method | Creates New List? | Safe for Nested Lists? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `new_list = my_list` | \u274c No (reference) | \u274c No |\n| `my_list.copy()` | \u2705 Yes | \u26a0\ufe0f Partially |\n| `my_list[:]` | \u2705 Yes | \u26a0\ufe0f Partially |\n| `list(my_list)` | \u2705 Yes | \u26a0\ufe0f Partially |\n| `copy.deepcopy(my_list)` | \u2705 Yes | \u2705 Yes |\n\nAlways be aware of whether you are working with a reference or a copy, as this helps you avoid unexpected changes to data. When in doubt, make a copy \u2014 if you're not sure whether a function will modify the data you pass, it's safer to pass a copy.",
